<title>MADAGASCAR: Madagascar vets warn of risk of mad cow disease.</title>
<headline>Madagascar vets warn of risk of mad cow disease.</headline>
<dateline>ANTANANARIVO 1997-02-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>Madagascar's veterinary surgeons have warned against a plan to import dairy cattle from the Netherlands because of the risk of bringing mad cow disease into the Indian Ocean island.</p>
<p>The veterinarians' national association published a declaration in Antananarivo on Sunday opposing moves to import 200 dairy cattle to reinforce the country's own dairy herds.</p>
<p>Dr Rakoto Rabevazaha, president of the association, told Madagascar radio the country was free from a range of diseases that exist elsewhere.</p>
<p>In April last year the Dutch started to slaughter 64,000 imported British calves over fear of bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), known as mad cow disease.</p>
</text>
